 17%|█▋        | 166/1000 [00:48<02:56,  4.73it/s]INFO 09-15 16:07:44 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-89b1e0e9b12b4db689e5a3b3c31f5af9-0: prompt: "Autoregressive (AR) is a time series forecasting technique that uses previous observations as input to make predictions. In an autoregressive model, the output variable (i.e., the variable we want to predict) is regressed on its own previous values, as well as on lagged values of other variables that might be affecting it.\n\nAn autoregressive model of order p, or AR(p), uses the p previous observations to predict the current observation. The general equation for an AR(p) model is:\n\ny(t) = c + φ1*y(t-1) + φ2*y(t-2) + … + φp\\*y(t-p) + ε(t)\n\nwhere y(t) is the value of the variable at time t, c is a constant, φ1 to φp are the coefficients of the previous observations, ε(t) is the error term (i.e., the difference between the predicted value and the actual value), and p is the order of the autoregression.\n\nThe goal of an autoregressive model is to estimate the coefficients φ1 to φp, as well as the constant c, that minimize the sum of squared errors between the predicted values and the actual values. This is typically done using a method called least squares estimation.\n\nAR models can be extended to include exogenous variables, which are external factors that can influence the time series being predicted. These models are called autoregressive integrated moving average with exogenous inputs (ARIMAX) models.\n\nPython provides a number of libraries for time series forecasting, including statsmodels, scikit-learn, and Prophet. Here is an example of how to implement an AR model in Python using the statsmodels library:\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.api as sm\n\n# Load the data\ndata = pd.read_csv('data.csv', index_col='Date', parse_dates=True)\n\n# Create the model\nmodel = sm.tsa.AR(data['Value'])\n\n# Fit the model\nresults = model.fit()\n\n# Make predictions\npredictions = results.predict(start='2022-01-01', end='2022-12-31')\n```\nIn this example, we first load the time series data into a Pandas DataFrame, then create an AR model using the statsmodels library. We then fit the model to the data and use it to make predictions for the year 2022.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=8, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 3713, 663, 106430, 568, 1425, 236768, 563, 496, 990, 3605, 51875, 9939, 600, 6178, 3527, 12505, 618, 2744, 531, 1386, 22056, 236761, 799, 614, 91102, 106430, 2028, 236764, 506, 3938, 6471, 568, 236747, 236761, 236744, 1126, 506, 6471, 692, 1461, 531, 4773, 236768, 563, 1157, 7461, 580, 1061, 1852, 3527, 2979, 236764, 618, 1388, 618, 580, 174002, 2979, 529, 1032, 7016, 600, 2473, 577, 21574, 625, 236761, 108, 2267, 91102, 106430, 2028, 529, 1900, 510, 236764, 653, 8886, 236769, 236758, 779, 6178, 506, 510, 3527, 12505, 531, 4773, 506, 1873, 15412, 236761, 669, 2870, 4901, 573, 614, 8886, 236769, 236758, 236768, 2028, 563, 236787, 108, 236762, 236769, 236745, 236768, 578, 505, 900, 20542, 236770, 236829, 236762, 236769, 236745, 236772, 236770, 236768, 900, 20542, 236778, 236829, 236762, 236769, 236745, 236772, 236778, 236768, 900, 7881, 900, 20542, 236758, 176418, 236762, 236769, 236745, 236772, 236758, 236768, 900, 5722, 236769, 236745, 236768, 108, 3350, 570, 236769, 236745, 236768, 563, 506, 1550, 529, 506, 6471, 657, 990, 494, 236764, 505, 563, 496, 4512, 236764, 20542, 236770, 531, 20542, 236758, 659, 506, 15841, 529, 506, 3527, 12505, 236764, 5722, 236769, 236745, 236768, 563, 506, 3165, 1941, 568, 236747, 236761, 236744, 1126, 506, 4954, 1534, 506, 