 26%|██▋       | 265/1000 [01:18<04:03,  3.02it/s]INFO 09-15 16:08:14 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-ac70ed73643d4509a1e86293c9e9e447-0: prompt: 'Apologies for the oversight. Here\'s an updated example with the filtering by ID located in the email body.\n\nFirst, let\'s modify the `email_producer.py` script to extract the email ID from the email body and use it as the partition key:\n```python\nimport re\nimport json\n\nasync def get_email_id(message):\n    # Extract the email ID from the email body\n    text_part = message.text_part.get_payload().decode(message.text_part.charset)\n    match = re.search(r\'ID:\\s*(\\w+)\', text_part)\n    return match.group(1) if match else None\n\nasync def on_new_email(producer, uid, mail):\n    raw_message = await mail.fetch([uid], [\'BODY[]\', \'FLAGS\'])\n    message = pyzmail36.PyzMessage.factory(raw_message[uid][b\'BODY[]\'])\n    email_id = await get_email_id(message)\n\n    if email_id:\n        subject = message.get_subject()\n        from_email = message.get_addresses(\'from\')\n\n        email_data = {\n            \'uid\': uid,\n            \'subject\': subject,\n            \'from\': from_email\n        }\n\n        # Send the email data to the Kafka topic using the email ID as the partition key\n        await producer.send_and_wait("email_topic", value=json.dumps(email_data).encode(), key=email_id.encode())\n\n# The rest of the email_producer.py remains the same\n```\nNow, create a custom consumer group for each `email_consumer.py` instance and filter the consumed messages based on the ID:\n```python\nimport json\n\n# Define the email ID that this instance should process\ninstance_email_id = \'some_id\'\n\nasync def consume_messages(consumer, mail):\n    async for msg in consumer:\n        # Get the email ID from the message key\n        email_id = msg.key.decode()\n\n        # Process the email only if the email ID matches the instance_email_id\n        if email_id == instance_email_id:\n            uid = int(json.loads(msg.value)[\'uid\'])\n            await process_email(mail, uid)\n\nasync def main(email, app_password, kafka_bootstrap_servers):\n    imap_server = \'outlook.office365.com\'\n    mail = aioimaplib.IMAP4_SSL(host=imap_server, timeout=10)\n    await mail.wait_hello_from_server()\n\n    await mail.login(email, app_password)\n    await mail.select(\'INBOX\')\n\n    consumer = AIOKafkaConsumer(\n        "email_topic",\n        bootstrap_servers=kafka_bootstrap_servers,\n        group_id=f"email_consumer_group_{instance_email_id}",\n        value_deserializer=lambda m: m.decode("utf-8"),\n        key_deserializer=lambda m: m.decode("utf-8")\n    )\n    await consumer.start()\n\n    try:\n        await consume_messages(consumer, mail)\n    finally:\n        await consumer.stop()\n\n# The rest of the email_consumer.py remains the same\n```\nWith this setup, each instance of `email_consumer.py` will only process emails with the specified email ID in the email body. You can run multiple instances with different values for `instance_email_id` to handle different subsets of emails. Note that the example assumes that the email body contains a line with the format `ID: some_id`. You should modify the `get_email_id` function in the `email_producer.py` script to match the actual format of the email ID in the email body.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=34, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 22342, 7378, 573, 506, 52233, 236761, 5715, 236789, 236751, 614, 10448, 2591, 607, 506, 35129, 684, 5853, 5628, 528, 506, 4553, 2742, 236761, 108, 7280, 236764, 1531, 236789, 236751, 17114, 50