INFO 09-15 16:08:47 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-5079321b28284a979e7f2065292f4c2f-0: prompt: 'To ensure that the right dependencies are installed and that the correct MongoDB connection string is used in production, follow these steps:\n\n1. Update `package.json` to include the `properties-reader` package in the `devDependencies` section:\n```json\n"devDependencies": {\n  "jest": "^27.4.5",\n  "mongodb-memory-server": "^7.6.3",\n  "properties-reader": "^2.2.0"\n}\n```\n1. Modify the `db.js` file to read the `/etc/cloudfinder/common.properties` file in production and use the provided connection string. In the testing environment, it will default to the in-memory MongoDB instance.\n```javascript\nconst { MongoClient } = require(\'mongodb\');\nconst PropertiesReader = require(\'properties-reader\');\n\nconst getConnectionString = () => {\n  if (process.env.NODE_ENV === \'production\') {\n    const properties = PropertiesReader(\'/etc/cloudfinder/common.properties\');\n    return properties.get(\'mongo.connectionStringUri\');\n  } else {\n    return \'mongodb://localhost:27017/\';\n  }\n};\n\nconst uri = getConnectionString();\nconst client = new MongoClient(uri, { useNewUrlParser: true, useUnifiedTopology: true });\n\n// Existing code for getFailingContainers function\n```\n1. In your `index.js` file, set the `NODE_ENV` environment variable to `\'production\'` when running the script in a production environment. In the testing environment, the `NODE_ENV` variable will not be set, and the code will default to the in-memory MongoDB instance.\n```javascript\nprocess.env.NODE_ENV = \'production\';\n\n// Existing code for running the application\n```\nNow, when running the application in a production environment, it will read the MongoDB connection string from the `/etc/cloudfinder/common.properties` file. In the testing environment, it will use the in-memory MongoDB instance.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_pena