 54%|█████▍    | 542/1000 [02:32<01:00,  7.58it/s]INFO 09-15 16:09:28 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-f2a74d550c5341a9814d12942ed79328-0: prompt: "The query I provided would retrieve the correct data if a company is linked to multiple sectors, however, each row returned will be a duplicate of the activity with the exception of the sector column, this is because the query is joining the activities table with the company table, then joining the company table with the sectors table, so for each sector a company is linked to, there will be a duplicate row for each activity that is linked to that company.\n\nIf you expect a company to have multiple sectors, you can use the GROUP\\_CONCAT function to concatenate all the sector names in one column. Here's an example:\n```\nSELECT \n    a.ID,\n    a.Type,\n    a.Description,\n    a.Date,\n    c.Name as 'CompanyName',\n    co.Address as 'CompanyAddress',\n    GROUP_CONCAT(s.SectorName) as 'SectorName',\n    con.FirstName as 'ContactFirstName',\n    con.LastName as 'ContactLastName',\n    con.Email as 'ContactEmail',\n    con.Phone as 'ContactPhone'\nFROM \n    Activities a\n    LEFT JOIN Companies c ON a.CompanyID = c.ID\n    LEFT JOIN CompanySectors cs ON c.ID = cs.CompanyID\n    LEFT JOIN Sectors s ON cs.SectorID = s.ID\n    LEFT JOIN Contacts con ON a.ContactID = con.ID\nGROUP BY\n    a.ID,\n    c.ID\n```\nWith this query, each row returned will be unique to the activity, and the sector column will contain a concatenated list of all sectors the company is linked to separated by a comma.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=27, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_a