INFO 09-15 16:10:27 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-943ba6269825434c9a2022f1aa666d64-0: prompt: 'HttpAdapters in Spring Boot are used to provide a consistent way of interacting with HTTP endpoints, such as RESTful web services. Here are some best practices for using HttpAdapters in Spring Boot:\n\n1. Use interfaces to define HttpAdapter implementations: Define an interface that defines the methods for each HTTP endpoint you want to interact with, and then provide one or more implementations of the interface using HttpAdapters. This approach allows you to easily switch between different implementations, such as different versions of an API, or different implementations for different environments.\n2. Use HttpAdapterBuilder to configure HttpAdapters: Spring Boot provides an HttpAdapterBuilder class that can be used to configure HttpAdapters. Use HttpAdapterBuilder to set default headers, configure connection pools, or configure retry policies.\n3. Use a central place to manage HttpAdapter instances: Rather than creating HttpAdapter instances manually, consider creating them using a factory or builder pattern in a central place in your application. This makes it easier to manage HttpAdapter instances, such as configuring them with default headers, and allows you to easily switch between different implementations.\n4. Keep HttpAdapter interfaces simple and focused: Use small, focused interfaces for HttpAdapter implementations that are easy to understand and use. Avoid overloading interfaces with too many methods or unnecessary complexity.\n5. Use HttpAdapter tests to verify API contracts: When using HttpAdapters to interact with external APIs, use tests to verify that the HttpAdapter implementations are correctly implementing the API contracts. This includes verifying expected HTTP status codes, headers, and response body formats.\n6. Keep HttpAdapter configuration separate from business logic: Avoid hardcoding HttpAdapter configuration values in business logic. Instead, use properties files or environment variables to configure HttpAdapter instances. This makes it easier to switch between different implementations and configurations, without affecting business logic.\n\nBy following these best practices, you can use HttpAdapters effectively in your Spring Boot application, providing a consistent and reliable way of interacting with HTTP endpoints.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=15, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 7004, 186207, 1826, 528, 8715, 28730, 659, 1456, 531, 2847, 496, 9958, 1595, 529, 37860, 607, 24644, 61925, 236764, 1288, 618, 48564, 1275, 4108, 3019, 236761, 5715, 659, 1070, 1791, 10154, 573, 1699, 16697, 186207, 1826, 528, 8715, 28730, 236787, 108, 236770, 236761, 6890, 33066, 531, 8540, 16697, 15050, 61949, 236787, 31238, 614, 7937, 600, 23669, 506, 4615, 573, 1546, 24644, 31466, 611, 1461, 531, 12740, 607, 236764, 532, 1299, 2847, 886, 653, 919, 61949, 529, 506, 7937, 1699, 16697, 186207, 1826, 236761, 1174, 4313, 5862, 611, 531, 5583, 5702, 1534, 1607, 61949, 236764, 1288, 618, 1607, 14273, 529, 614, 9299, 236764, 653, 1607, 61949, 573, 1607, 18567, 236761, 107, 236778, 236761, 6890, 16697, 15050, 8493, 531, 26955, 16697, 186207, 1826, 236787, 8715, 28730, 4728, 614, 16697, 15050, 8493, 1012, 600, 740, 577, 1456, 531, 26955, 16697, 186207, 1826, 236761, 6890, 16697, 15050, 8493, 531, 1076, 3678, 18907, 236764, 26955, 5603, 36513, 236764, 653, 26955, 52312, 10034, 236761, 107, 236800, 236761, 6890, 496, 6082, 1977, 531, 9688, 16697, 15050, 16787, 236787, 31373, 1082, 7107, 16697, 15050, 16787, 24473, 236764, 1966,