 79%|███████▉  | 789/1000 [03:37<00:35,  5.95it/s]INFO 09-15 16:10:33 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-f326e24ffe2c484c8005ea8dc51915ee-0: prompt: 'A Document Type Definition (DTD) is a way to define the structure and content of an XML document. It specifies the elements and attributes that can appear in the document, as well as the relationships between them.\n\nHere is an example of a DTD for the simple XML file I showed you earlier:\n```\n<!DOCTYPE catalog [\n  <!ELEMENT catalog (book*)>\n  <!ELEMENT book (author, title, genre, price, publish_date, description)>\n  <!ATTLIST book id ID #REQUIRED>\n  <!ELEMENT author (#PCDATA)>\n  <!ELEMENT title (#PCDATA)>\n  <!ELEMENT genre (#PCDATA)>\n  <!ELEMENT price (#PCDATA)>\n  <!ELEMENT publish_date (#PCDATA)>\n  <!ELEMENT description (#PCDATA)>\n]>\n```\nThis DTD defines the structure of the XML file, stating that the root element is `catalog`, and that it contains zero or more `book` elements. Each `book` element must contain the elements `author`, `title`, `genre`, `price`, `publish_date`, and `description`, in that order. The `book` element also has an attribute called `id`, which is of type `ID` and is required.\n\nThe `#PCDATA` notation indicates that the elements contain parsed character data, which means that the data will be treated as text and any XML markup will be ignored.\n\nYou can use a DTD to validate an XML document to ensure that it is well-formed and conforms to the specified structure. This can be useful for ensuring the integrity and consistency of your data.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=13, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None