INFO 09-15 16:10:43 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-6caeb2d350224ca697c4e530962c25f3-0: prompt: "The code you provided is responsible for setting up the SignalR hub in your ASP.NET Core application.\n`app.UseEndpoints` is a method provided by the ASP.NET Core framework that sets up the endpoints for the application.\n`MapHub` method is used to configure a SignalR hub, it maps the hub to a specific route and it also accepts an options delegate that allows you to configure the hub.\n\nIn this code, the hub is being mapped to the route defined by the variable `signalEndPoint` and the `options.Transports` property is being set to use WebSockets transport.\nThe options.Transports property is used to specify which transport protocols the hub should use to connect to the clients. By setting it to HttpTransportType.WebSockets, the hub will use WebSockets as the preferred transport protocol, this mean that if WebSockets is supported by the client, it will be used for communication, if not it will fallback to other available transports.\n\nIt's also important to note that you should also configure the WebSockets transport in the server, if you want to use it, this can be done through the UseWebSockets method in the configure method of the Startup class.\nIt's also good to note that there is no mention of any backplane configuration in this code, if you want to use a backplane, you'll need to configure it separately.\n\nThis is the code that sets up the SignalR hub on the server side and it's not responsible for initiating the connection from the client side.\nTo initiate a SignalR hub connection in a .NET application and handle reconnection, you should use the `HubConnection` class provided by the SignalR library as I explained in my previous answer.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_outp