 85%|████████▌ | 854/1000 [03:50<00:23,  6.34it/s]INFO 09-15 16:10:46 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-f0d60295f276479981f9be5b48bbb317-0: prompt: 'Yes, Terraform can help with packaging and deploying your `ML.NET` assembly and any necessary dependencies to Azure. Terraform is an open-source infrastructure as code tool that enables you to define and manage your infrastructure and resources as code, using a simple declarative language.\n\nWith Terraform, you can define an Azure resource group, storage account, and any other required resources, and then use a tool like PowerShell or Azure CLI to package and upload your `ML.NET` assembly and dependencies to Azure Storage. You can then reference the storage account and container in your Terraform configuration to deploy the resources to Azure.\n\nHere\'s an example of a Terraform configuration that deploys an Azure Storage account and uploads a local file to the account:\n```java\nprovider "azurerm" {\n  features {}\n}\n\nresource "azurerm_resource_group" "example" {\n  name     = "example-resource-group"\n  location = "eastus"\n}\n\nresource "azurerm_storage_account" "example" {\n  name                     = "examplestorageaccount"\n  resource_group_name      = azurerm_resource_group.example.name\n  location                 = azurerm_resource_group.example.location\n  account_tier             = "Standard"\n  account_replication_type = "LRS"\n}\n\nresource "azurerm_storage_container" "example" {\n  name                  = "examplecontainer"\n  storage_account_name  = azurerm_storage_account.example.name\n  container_access_type = "private"\n}\n\nresource "azurerm_storage_blob" "example" {\n  name                   = "exampleblob"\n  storage_account_name   = azurerm_storage_account.example.name\n  storage_container_name = azurerm_storage_container.example.name\n  type                   = "Block"\n  source                 = "path/to/MyClass.dll"\n}\n```\nThis Terraform configuration creates an Azure resource group, a storage account, a storage container, and a storage blob. The storage blob uploads a local file named `MyClass.dll` to the storage container. You can replace the `MyClass.dll` file with your `ML.NET` assembly file and any necessary dependencies.\n\nOnce you have uploaded your `ML.NET` assembly and dependencies to Azure Storage, you can reference the storage account and container in your Terraform configuration to deploy the resources that use the assembly, such as an Azure Function or an Azure Container Instance.\n\nNote that this is just a simple example, and you may need to modify the configuration to suit your specific requirements. You will also need to ensure that you have the necessary permissions and credentials to deploy the resources to Azure using Terraform.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=13, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 10784, 236764, 163735, 740, 1601, 607, 16593, 532, 76702, 822, 2165, 3620, 236761, 18851, 236929, 14510, 532, 1027, 4127, 29176, 531, 27169, 236761, 163735, 563, 614, 1932, 236772, 6265, 12308, 618, 3393, 5904, 600, 17630, 611, 531, 8540, 532, 9688, 822, 12308, 532, 5405, 618, 3393, 236764, 1699, 496, 3606, 160180, 5192, 236761, 108, 3497, 163735, 236764, 611, 740, 8540, 614, 27169, 6511, 2299, 236764, 6310, 2881, 236764, 532, 1027, 1032, 3149, 5405, 236764, 532, 1299, 1161, 496, 5904, 1133, 122316, 653, 27169, 71597, 531, 8289, 532, 13612, 822, 2165, 3620, 236761, 18851, 236929, 14510, 532, 29176, 531, 27169, 25795, 236761, 1599, 740, 1299, 5577, 506, 6310, 2881, 532, 9714, 528, 822, 163735, 9831, 531, 15440, 506, 5405, 531, 27169, 236761,