 88%|████████▊ | 885/1000 [03:59<00:33,  3.45it/s]INFO 09-15 16:10:55 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-6a67172dc85a40248c4bb4e750ae5edf-0: prompt: 'It is not necessary to acquire a lock for each node when traversing the tree in the code you provided. In fact, acquiring a lock for each node in the tree could lead to performance issues and could potentially cause a deadlock if multiple threads try to acquire locks on nodes at the same time.\n\nInstead, you can use a single lock to synchronize access to the tree. For example:\n```\npublic class Test {\n    // ...\n    final Object treeLock = new Object();\n\n    static class Node {\n        // ...\n        boolean isLocked = false;\n\n        public boolean lock(int uuid) throws InterruptedException {\n            synchronized (treeLock) {\n                Node curr = node.parent;\n                while (curr != null) {\n                    if (curr.isLocked == true) {\n                        return false;\n                    }\n                    curr = curr.parent;\n                }\n                // ...\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n\n        public boolean unlock(int uuid) throws InterruptedException {\n            synchronized (treeLock) {\n                // ...\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n\n        public boolean upgrade(int uuid) throws InterruptedException {\n            synchronized (treeLock) {\n                // ...\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\nIn this example, the `treeLock` field is an object that is used as a lock to synchronize access to the tree. The `synchronized` block is used to acquire the lock and to ensure that only one thread can modify the tree at a time.\n\nUsing a single lock to synchronize access to the tree can be a good way to avoid the overhead of acquiring a lock for each node in the tree and can help prevent potential deadlock situations.\n\nI hope this helps. Please let me know if you have any other questions.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=33, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 1509, 563, 711, 4127, 531, 22317, 496, 6623, 573, 1546, 5349, 1056, 137577, 506, 5028, 528, 506, 3393, 611, 3847, 236761, 799, 1707, 236764, 44708, 496, 6623, 573, 1546, 5349, 528, 506, 5028, 1451, 2080, 531, 3736, 4342, 532, 1451, 14846, 4400, 496, 141145, 768, 5065, 25843, 2056, 531, 22317, 41226, 580, 13653, 657, 506, 1638, 990, 236761, 108, 40725, 236764, 611, 740, 1161, 496, 3161, 6623, 531, 124691, 2802, 531, 506, 5028, 236761, 1701, 2591, 236787, 107, 2717, 107, 2153, 1012, 5803, 642, 107, 140, 715, 3729, 107, 140, 10218, 8196, 5028, 20954, 578, 861, 8196, 1086, 108, 140, 6990, 1012, 14741, 642, 107, 144, 715, 3729, 107, 144, 23470, 563, 108189, 578, 2416, 236793, 108, 144, 2153, 8538, 6623, 236769, 720, 53817, 236768, 9160, 4471, 19625, 642, 107, 148, 176861, 568, 10750, 20954, 236768, 642, 107, 152, 4740, 25108, 578, 5349, 236761, 5432, 236793, 107, 152, 6858, 568, 24902, 2843, 2303, 236768, 642, 107, 156, 584, 568, 24902, 236761, 511, 108189, 1251, 1847, 236768, 642, 107, 160, 2060, 2416, 236793, 107, 156, 236783, 107, 156, 24902, 578, 25108, 236761, 5432, 236793, 107, 152, 236783, 107, 152, 715, 3729, 107, 152, 2060, 1847, 236793, 107, 148, 236783, 107, 144, 236783, 108, 144, 2153, 8538, 24486, 236769, 720, 53817, 236768, 9160, 4471, 19625, 642, 107, 148, 176861, 568, 10750, 20954, 236768, 642, 107, 152, 715, 3729, 107, 152, 2060, 1847, 236793, 107, 148, 236783, 107, 144, 236783, 108, 144, 2153, 8538, 19260, 236769, 720, 53817, 236768, 9160, 4471, 19625, 642, 