INFO 09-15 16:12:23 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-e796f8606d844e80b78550048db48367-0: prompt: 'If the input to the Kernel Ridge Regression (KRR) model is a vector of inverse distances between a reference point and a set of neighboring points, the derivative of the predicted output with respect to the reference point can be obtained similarly to the case of two points in 3D space.\n\nAssuming that the KRR model uses a Gaussian kernel, the predicted output $y$ for a given vector of inverse distances $\\mathbf{f}$ can be expressed as:\n\ny=∑i=1Nαik(f,fi)y = \\sum\\_{i=1}^{N} \\alpha\\_i k(\\mathbf{f}, \\mathbf{f}\\_i)y=i=1∑N\u200bαi\u200bk(f,fi\u200b)where $N$ is the number of training samples, $\\alpha\\_i$ are the coefficients obtained during training, $\\mathbf{f}\\_i$ are the vectors of inverse distances for the training samples, and $k(\\mathbf{f}, \\mathbf{f}\\_i)$ is the Gaussian kernel function:\n\nk(f,fi)=exp\u2061(−∥f−fi∥22σ2)k(\\mathbf{f}, \\mathbf{f}\\_i) = \\exp \\left( -\\frac{\\|\\mathbf{f}-\\mathbf{f}\\_i\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2} \\right)k(f,fi\u200b)=exp(−2σ2∥f−fi\u200b∥2\u200b)where $\\sigma$ is the kernel width and $|\\cdot|$ denotes the Euclidean norm.\n\nTo compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to the reference point, say $\\mathbf{a}$, we first need to express each component of $\\mathbf{f}$ as a function of $\\mathbf{a}$ and the coordinates of the neighboring points. The inverse distance $f\\_i$ between the reference point $\\mathbf{a}$ and a neighboring point $\\mathbf{b}\\_i$ can be written as:\n\nfi(a,bi)=1d(a,bi)=1(bi−a)⋅(bi−a)f\\_i(\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}\\_i) = \\frac{1}{d(\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}\\_i)} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(\\mathbf{b}\\_i-\\mathbf{a})\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\_i-\\mathbf{a})}}fi\u200b(a,bi\u200b)=d(a,bi\u200b)1\u200b=(bi\u200b−a)⋅(bi\u200b−a)\u200b1\u200bTaking the partial derivative of $y$ with respect to the $j$-th component of $\\mathbf{a}$ yields:\n\n∂y∂aj=∑i=1Nαi∂k(f,fi)∂fj∂fi∂aj=−∑i=1Nαik(f,fi)(bij−aj)di3fj−fijσ2\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial a\\_j} &= \\sum\\_{i=1}^{N} \\alpha\\_i \\frac{\\partial k(\\mathbf{f}, \\mathbf{f}\\_i)}{\\partial f\\_j} \\frac{\\partial f\\_i}{\\partial a\\_j} \\\\\n&= -\\sum\\_{i=1}^{N} \\alpha\\_i k(\\mathbf{f}, \\mathbf{f}\\_i) \\frac{(b\\_{ij}-a\\_j)}{d\\_i^3} \\frac{f\\_j-f\\_{ij}}{\\sigma^2}\n\\end{aligned}∂aj\u200b∂y\u200b\u200b=i=1∑N\u200bαi\u200b∂fj\u200b∂k(f,fi\u200b)\u200b∂aj\u200b∂fi\u200b\u200b=−i=1∑N\u200bαi\u200bk(f,fi\u200b)di3\u200b(bij\u200b−aj\u200b)\u200bσ2fj\u200b−fij\u200b\u200b\u200bwhere $d\\_i = d(\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}\\_i)$ is the Euclidean distance between the reference point $\\mathbf{a}$ and the neighboring point $\\mathbf{b}*i$, $b*{ij}$ is the $j$-th component of $\\mathbf{b}*i$, $f*{ij}$ is the inverse distance between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\_i$ along the $j$-th dimension, and $f\\_j$ is the $j$-th component of the input vector $\\mathbf{f}$.\n\nTherefore, the derivative of the KRR model with a Gaussian kernel trained on a vector of inverse distances with respect to the reference point is also a linear combination of the kernel values, the coefficients obtained during training', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=9, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 2859, 506, 2744, 531, 506, 114999, 28372, 77361, 568, 236855, 39907, 236768, 2028, 563, 496, 3550, 529, 19141, 22086, 1534, 496, 5577, 1523, 532, 496, 1076, 529, 38477, 3298, 236764, 506, 17407, 529, 506, 15282, 3938, 607, 2833, 531, 506, 5577, 1523, 740, 577, 4597, 24977, 531, 506, 1624, 529, 1156, 3298, 528, 236743, 236800, 236796, 2557, 236761, 108, 83033