INFO 09-15 16:12:55 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-267e086eba90415480716c4a4b139a96-0: prompt: 'Sure, here is an example of the input data and the expected output based on the SQL script I provided earlier:\n\nAssume we have six tables with the following data:\n\n1. `orders` table:\n\n| order\\_id | customer\\_id | order\\_date |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 1 | 2022-01-01 |\n| 2 | 2 | 2022-02-15 |\n| 3 | 3 | 2022-03-10 |\n| 4 | 1 | 2022-03-20 |\n\n2. `order_items` table:\n\n| order\\_id | product\\_id | quantity | price |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 1 | 2 | 100 |\n| 1 | 2 | 3 | 50 |\n| 2 | 1 | 1 | 100 |\n| 3 | 3 | 2 | 75 |\n| 4 | 2 | 2 | 50 |\n\n3. `products` table:\n\n| product\\_id | product\\_name |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Product A |\n| 2 | Product B |\n| 3 | Product C |\n\n4. `customers` table:\n\n| customer\\_id | customer\\_name | region\\_id |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Customer X | 1 |\n| 2 | Customer Y | 2 |\n| 3 | Customer Z | 1 |\n\n5. `sales_regions` table:\n\n| region\\_id | region\\_name |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Region A |\n| 2 | Region B |\n\n6. `dates` table:\n\n| date |\n| --- |\n| 2022-01-01 |\n| 2022-02-01 |\n| 2022-03-01 |\n\nAfter running the SQL script to populate the `sales_summary` table, the expected output would be:\n\n| region | product | month | total\\_sales |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Region A | Product A | 2022-01-01 | 200 |\n| Region A | Product B | 2022-03-01 | 100 |\n| Region B | Product A | 2022-02-01 | 100 |\n| Region A | Product C | 2022-03-01 | 150 |\n\nThis output shows the total sales for each combination of region, product, and month, based on the data in the `orders`, `order_items`, `products`, `customers`, `sales_regions`, and `dates` tables. The `sales_summary` table contains this same information, but in a denormalized format that can be more efficient for reporting purposes.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=