 32%|███▏      | 315/1000 [00:49<01:40,  6.79it/s]INFO 09-15 16:12:57 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-0b90fcc67a73474b92a5db7ab1d3f4e4-0: prompt: 'In case of a cyber security incident involving sensitive PII data exfiltration and extortion, an Australian company providing an online conveyancing platform would need to interact with law enforcement and relevant organizations in a timely and responsible manner. The specific approach would depend on the nature and scale of the incident, but here are some general guidelines:\n\n1. Contact law enforcement - In case of a cybercrime, such as extortion or data theft, the company should contact law enforcement as soon as possible. The Australian Federal Police (AFP) is the primary law enforcement agency that investigates cybercrime in Australia.\n2. Notify relevant organizations - Depending on the nature and scale of the incident, the company may also need to notify relevant organizations such as the Australian Cyber Security Centre (ACSC), the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner (OAIC), and the Australian Securities and Investments Commission (ASIC).\n3. Cooperate with law enforcement - The company should cooperate with law enforcement in their investigation of the incident, providing any relevant information and access to systems as needed.\n4. Notify affected individuals - The company may be required to notify affected individuals in case of a data breach under the Notifiable Data Breaches (NDB) scheme.\n5. Seek legal counsel - The company may want to seek legal counsel to determine their legal obligations and liabilities in case of a cyber security incident.\n\nIt is important for the company to act quickly and responsibly to minimize the impact of the incident and to comply with legal requirements. The specific organizations and authorities that the company needs to inform and work with would depend on the specific circumstances of the incident.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequ