 36%|███▌      | 360/1000 [00:55<01:08,  9.40it/s]INFO 09-15 16:13:03 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-ce51b57430c4409b9f9fa0590eb9d382-0: prompt: 'To use the `bytes.decode` method to decode a column in a Pandas dataframe, you can use the `apply` method of the dataframe and pass it a custom function that decodes the bytes objects in the column using the "utf-8" character encoding.\n\nHere\'s an example of how you can do this:\n```\ndef decode_column(text):\n    return text.decode(\'utf-8\')\n\ndf[\'column_name\'] = df[\'column_name\'].apply(decode_column)\n```\nThis will apply the `decode_column` function to each cell in the "column\\_name" column of the dataframe and update the cell with the decoded text.\n\nAlternatively, you can use the `ftfy` library to automatically detect and fix the incorrect character encoding. Here\'s an example of how you can use it:\n```\nimport ftfy\n\ndef fix_column(text):\n    fixed_text = ftfy.fix_text(text)\n    return fixed_text\n\ndf[\'column_name\'] = df[\'column_name\'].apply(fix_column)\n```\nThis will apply the `fix_column` function to each cell in the "column\\_name" column of the dataframe and update the cell with the fixed text.\n\nKeep in mind that these examples assume that the bytes objects in the "column\\_name" column were encoded with the "utf-8" character encoding. If the bytes objects were encoded with a different character encoding, you will need to adjust the decoding step accordingly.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=11, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 2021, 1161, 506, 2165, 18222, 236761, 22360, 236929, 1996, 531, 