 53%|█████▎    | 528/1000 [01:16<01:13,  6.44it/s]INFO 09-15 16:13:24 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-748ec9894b654f5d9335a30f2040b24b-0: prompt: 'The error message you are seeing indicates that the value you are returning from your endpoint is not a valid dictionary.\n\nIn FastAPI, you can use Pydantic models to define the request and response data of your API endpoints. When you return a Pydantic model instance from an endpoint, FastAPI automatically serializes it to JSON and returns it as the response.\n\nTo fix the error, you need to make sure that the value you are returning is a valid dictionary or a Pydantic model instance.\n\nHere\'s an example of how to define a Pydantic model for a list of dictionaries:\n```python\nfrom typing import List\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclass User(BaseModel):\n    id: int\n    name: str\n\nclass UserList(BaseModel):\n    users: List[User]\n```\nIn this example, we define a `User` Pydantic model with two fields: `id` and `name`. We also define a new model `UserList` that contains a list of `User` objects.\n\nTo return a list of dictionaries in a response, you can create a new instance of the `UserList` model and set the `users` field to a list of `User` objects. For example:\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom typing import List\nfrom app.models import User, UserList\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get("/users", response_model=UserList)\ndef read_users():\n    users = [\n        {"id": 1, "name": "Alice"},\n        {"id": 2, "name": "Bob"},\n        {"id": 3, "name": "Charlie"}\n    ]\n    user_objects = [User.parse_obj(user) for user in users]\n    return UserList(users=user_objects)\n```\nIn this example, we define a new endpoint that returns a list of users. We create a list of dictionaries representing the users and then use a list comprehension to parse each dictionary into a `User` model instance. We then create a new `UserList` model instance and set the `users` field to the list of `User` objects. We return the `UserList` instance as the response.\n\nBy returning a Pydantic model instance, FastAPI will automatically serialize it to JSON and return it as the response.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=14, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 818, 3165, 3618, 611, 659, 9333, 11387, 600, 506, 1550, 611, 659, 16228, 699, 822, 31466, 563, 711, 496, 4341, 19086, 236761, 108, 902, 140284, 236764, 611, 740, 1161, 593, 2274, 8117, 4681, 531, 8540, 506, 2864, 532, 3072, 1262, 529, 822, 9299, 61925, 236761, 3026, 611, 994, 496, 593, 2274, 8117, 2028, 5705, 699, 614, 31466, 236764, 140284, 11269, 14722, 5411, 625, 531, 10434, 532, 7623, 625, 618, 506, 3072, 236761, 108, 2021, 7562, 506, 3165, 236764, 611, 1202, 531, 1386, 2889, 600, 506, 1550, 611, 659, 16228, 563, 496, 4341, 19086, 653, 496, 593, 2274, 8117, 2028, 5705, 236761, 108, 8291, 236789, 236751, 614, 2591, 529, 1217, 531, 8540, 496, 593, 2274, 8117, 2028, 573, 496, 1694, 529, 114114, 236787, 107, 2717, 6719, 107, 2543, 28751, 1419, 4361, 107, 2543, 510, 2274, 8117, 1419, 229103, 108, 1999, 7486, 236769, 8728, 4968, 1473, 107, 140, 547, 236787, 801, 107, 140, 1201, 236787, 1540, 108, 1999, 7486, 1613, 236769, 8728, 4968, 1473, 107, 140, 12641, 236787, 4361, 236840, 2887, 236842, 107, 2717, 107, 902, 672, 2591, 236764, 692, 8540, 496, 2165, 2887, 236929, 593, 2274, 8117, 2028, 607, 1156, 6192, 236787, 2165, 547, 236929, 532, 2165, 1201, 21233, 1191, 992, 8540, 496, 861, 2028, 2165, 2887, 1613, 236929, 600, 6097, 496, 1694, 529, 2165, 2887, 236929, 7751, 236761, 108, 2021, 994, 496, 1694, 529, 114114, 528, 496, 3072, 236764, 611, 