INFO 09-15 16:13:32 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-76014f0e5968414ba2f5ad214538c9ec-0: prompt: "Sure, I'd be happy to elaborate on each of the techniques I mentioned for handling large and complex data in ML Ops:\n\n1. Distributed Computing: ML Ops specialists use distributed computing frameworks like Apache Spark and Hadoop to process and analyze large data sets in parallel across multiple nodes. These frameworks allow data to be split into smaller chunks and processed in parallel, which speeds up the processing time and reduces the need for large amounts of memory. In addition, distributed computing frameworks can be used to scale up or down as needed to handle changes in data volume.\n2. Data Versioning: ML Ops specialists use tools like Git and DVC to track changes to data sets and manage different versions of data. These tools allow ML Ops specialists to keep track of changes to data over time and to revert to previous versions if needed. In addition, data versioning can help ensure that different teams are using the same data sets and that data is properly documented and annotated.\n3. Data Pipelines: ML Ops specialists use tools like Apache Airflow and Luigi to create and manage data pipelines, which automate the movement of data between different systems and processes. Data pipelines can be used to move data between different stages of the ML workflow, such as data cleaning, feature engineering, and model training. In addition, data pipelines can be used to schedule and automate tasks, reducing the need for manual intervention.\n4. Cloud Storage: ML Ops specialists use cloud storage solutions like AWS S3, Google Cloud Storage, and Azure Blob Storage to store and access large data sets. Cloud storage solutions provide scalable, secure, and cost-effective storage for large data sets. In addition, cloud storage solutions can be integrated with other cloud-based tools and services, such as distributed computing frameworks and data pipelines.\n\nEach of these techniques is important for handling large and complex data in ML Ops, and ML Ops specialists may use a combination of these techniques depending on the specific needs of their organization and their ML workflows.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=17, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 49190, 236764, 564, 236789, 236753, 577, 5293, 531, 37263, 580, 1546, 529, 506, 8403, 564, 6950, 573, 12804, 2455, 532, 3996, 1262, 528, 20885, 115564, 236787, 108, 236770, 236761, 81829, 51581, 236787, 20885, 115564, 30835, 1161, 10861, 20124, 44362, 1133, 28092, 39846, 532, 190184, 531, 1657, 532, 18840, 2455, 1262, 7093, 528, 10616, 3418, 5065, 13653, 236761, 3143, 44362, 2174, 1262, 531, 577, 9918, 1131, 7100, 49145, 532, 18761, 528, 10616, 236764, 837, 23257, 872, 506, 8487, 990, 532, 17707, 506, 1202, 573, 2455, 12136, 529, 6571, 236761, 799, 2517, 236764, 10861, 20124, 44362, 740, 577, 1456, 531, 5559, 872, 653, 1679, 618, 4354, 531, 6060, 3731, 528, 1262, 5542, 236761, 107, 236778, 236761, 5774, 16811, 522, 236787, 20885, 115564, 30835, 1161, 6436, 1133, 21864, 532, 622, 15191, 531, 4087, 3731, 531, 1262, 7093, 532, 9688, 1607, 14273, 529, 1262, 236761, 3143, 6436, 2174, 20885, 115564, 30835, 531, 2514, 4087, 529, 3731, 531, 1262, 1024, 990, 532, 531, 56704, 531, 3527, 14273, 768, 4354, 236761, 799, 2517, 236764, 1262, 3567, 522, 740, 1601, 5330, 600, 1607, 7974, 659, 1699, 506, 1638, 1262, 7093, 532, 600, 1262, 563, 9668, 31037, 532, 89001, 236761, 107, 236800, 236761, 5774, 210698, 236787, 20885, 115564, 30835, 1161, 6436, 1133, 28092, 5661, 5101, 532, 100054, 531, 2619, 532, 9688,