 67%|██████▋   | 668/1000 [01:39<01:12,  4.59it/s]INFO 09-15 16:13:47 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-a711fd50491c43ebbd2685bf1bf8ee66-0: prompt: 'If you want to individually control access to each page and control in your ASP.NET MVC Core application, you can define each page and control as a resource and assign it a unique identifier. You can then use these identifiers in your external authorization system, such as Azure AD, to define the access policies for each resource.\n\nHere\'s an example of how you can define a resource in Azure AD:\n\n1. Define a resource: Define a resource in Azure AD that corresponds to a page or control in your ASP.NET MVC Core application. You can define the resource by adding a new application manifest file in the Azure Portal, and adding a new `resources` property to the manifest. The `resources` property is an array of objects that define each resource.\n\nHere\'s an example of how you can define a resource in the `resources` property:\n```json\n"resources": [\n  {\n    "resourceAppId": "your-application-client-id",\n    "resourceAccess": [\n      {\n        "id": "e7d262d2-17df-4268-8c08-7d21b9eac2b1",\n        "type": "Scope"\n      }\n    ],\n    "resourceDisplayName": "Page1",\n    "resourceName": "your-application-uri/page1"\n  }\n],\n```\nIn this example, the `resources` property contains one object that defines the "Page1" resource. The `resourceAppId` property is the client ID of your ASP.NET MVC Core application in Azure AD, and the `resourceName` property is the URI of the protected resource.\n\n2. Assign permissions: Assign permissions to each resource in Azure AD. You can assign permissions by adding them to the `resourceAccess` property of the resource object. The `resourceAccess` property is an array of objects that define the permissions for the resource.\n\nHere\'s an example of how you can assign permissions to the "Page1" resource in the `resourceAccess` property:\n```json\n"resourceAccess": [\n  {\n    "id": "e7d262d2-17df-4268-8c08-7d21b9eac2b1",\n    "type": "Scope"\n  }\n],\n```\nIn this example, the `id` property is the unique identifier for the permission, and the `type` property is set to `Scope`, which indicates that this is a permission scope.\n\n3. Define policies: Define policies that determine who has access to each resource and under what conditions. You can define policies using a policy language, such as XACML or JSON-based policies, in Azure AD or an external authorization system.\n\nHere\'s an example of how you can define a policy in Azure AD:\n```json\n{\n  "if": {\n    "allOf": [\n      {\n        "matches": {\n          "request.resource.name": "your-application-uri/page1"\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        "matches": {\n          "request.user.groups": "Page1_Readers"\n        }\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  "then": {\n    "permit": {}\n  },\n  "version": "1.0.0"\n}\n```\nIn this example, the policy grants access to the "Page1" resource if the user is a member of the "Page1\\_Readers" group.\n\n4. Implement policies: Once you have defined your policies, you can implement them in your ASP.NET MVC Core application using a custom authorization middleware. The middleware can intercept requests to protected resources and evaluate the user\'s attributes and other factors against the policies defined in the external authorization system.\n\nBy defining each page and control as a resource and assigning it a unique identifier, you can achieve fine-grained access control in your ASP.NET MVC Core application', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=14, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token