 67%|██████▋   | 669/1000 [01:39<01:05,  5.06it/s]INFO 09-15 16:13:47 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-1e4ff113985d429a826ad3b55ad20066-0: prompt: 'There are several statistical tests to test for autocorrelation and heteroscedasticity in time series data. Here are some common tests and how to perform them in R:\n\n1. Autocorrelation test:\nThe most common statistical test for autocorrelation is the Ljung-Box test, which tests the null hypothesis that there is no autocorrelation in the time series data up to a specified lag. The test calculates a test statistic and p-value, where a p-value less than the significance level indicates that the null hypothesis can be rejected in favor of the alternative hypothesis of autocorrelation.\n\nIn R, you can perform the Ljung-Box test using the Box.test() function. Here is an example:\n```r\n# Load the AirPassengers dataset and convert it to a time series object\ndata(AirPassengers)\ntsdata <- ts(AirPassengers, start = c(1949, 1), end = c(1960, 12), frequency = 12)\n\n# Perform the Ljung-Box test for autocorrelation up to lag 12\nBox.test(tsdata, lag = 12, type = "Ljung-Box")\n```\nThis code performs the Ljung-Box test for autocorrelation up to lag 12 on the AirPassengers dataset.\n\n1. Heteroscedasticity test:\nOne of the most common statistical tests for heteroscedasticity is the Breusch-Pagan test, which tests the null hypothesis that the variance of the residuals in a regression model is constant. The test calculates a test statistic and p-value, where a p-value less than the significance level indicates that the null hypothesis can be rejected in favor of the alternative hypothesis of heteroscedasticity.\n\nIn R, you can perform the Breusch-Pagan test using the bptest() function from the lmtest package. Here is an example:\n```r\n# Fit a linear regression model to the AirPassengers dataset\nfit <- lm(tsdata ~ time(tsdata))\n\n# Perform the Breusch-Pagan test for heteroscedasticity\nlibrary(lmtest)\nbptest(fit)\n```\nThis code fits a linear regression model to the AirPassengers dataset and performs the Breusch-Pagan test for heteroscedasticity on the residuals of the model.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=20, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 3810, 659, 3131, 18090, 7713, 531, 1594, 573, 158695, 532, 16860, 585, 1465, 3646, 665, 528, 990, 3605, 1262, 236761, 5715, 659, 1070, 3364, 7713, 532, 1217, 531, 2121, 1091, 528, 630, 236787, 108, 236770, 236761, 3919, 118698, 22653, 1594, 236787, 107, 818, 1346, 3364, 18090, 1594, 573, 158695, 563, 506, 639, 97155, 236772, 3406, 1594, 236764, 837, 7713, 506, 2303, 18754, 600, 993, 563, 951, 158695, 528, 506, 990, 3605, 1262, 872, 531, 496, 9155, 10783, 236761, 669, 1594, 62577, 496, 1594, 46615, 532, 510, 236772, 2394, 236764, 1298, 496, 510, 236772, 2394, 2344, 1082, 506, 17141, 1984, 11387, 600, 506, 2303, 18754, 740, 577, 21105, 528, 4664, 529, 506, 9302, 18754, 529, 158695, 236761, 108, 902, 630, 236764, 611, 740, 2121, 506, 639, 97155, 236772, 3406, 1594, 1699, 506, 9622, 236761, 2181, 825, 1292, 236761, 5715, 563, 614, 2591, 236787, 107, 2717, 236750, 107, 236865, 16959, 506, 5661, 124594, 15297, 532, 7352, 625, 531, 496, 990, 3605, 2495, 107, 1516, 236769, 17995, 124594, 236768, 107, 1412, 1516, 4778, 24696, 236769, 17995, 124594, 236764, 1502, 578, 505, 236769, 236770, 236819, 236812, 236819, 236764, 236743, 236770, 779, 1345, 578, 505, 236769, 236770, 236819, 236825, 236771, 236764, 236743, 236770, 236778, 779, 7132, 578, 236743, 236770, 236778, 236768, 108, 236865, 46531, 506, 639, 97155, 236772, 3406, 1594, 573, 158695, 872, 531, 10783, 