 73%|███████▎  | 728/1000 [01:46<00:24, 11.14it/s]INFO 09-15 16:13:54 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-e71e87b43fdc415ca45593678b7838f4-0: prompt: 'To give the Lambda function permissions to access the DynamoDB table, you will need to create an IAM policy and attach it to the IAM role that the Lambda function is using. Here is the Terraform code to do this:\n```\nresource "aws_iam_policy" "picas_dynamodb_access_policy" {\n  name        = "picas_dynamodb_access_policy"\n  description = "Policy granting access to the picas_table DynamoDB table"\n\n  policy = <<EOF\n{\n  "Version": "2012-10-17",\n  "Statement": [\n    {\n      "Action": [\n        "dynamodb:*"\n      ],\n      "Effect": "Allow",\n      "Resource": [\n        "${aws_dynamodb_table.picas_table.arn}"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\nEOF\n}\n\nresource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "picas_dynamodb_access_attachment" {\n  role       = "${aws_iam_role.picas_lambda_role.name}"\n  policy_arn = "${aws_iam_policy.picas_dynamodb_access_policy.arn}"\n}\n```\nThis code creates an IAM policy named "picas\\_dynamodb\\_access\\_policy" that grants the "dynamodb:\\*" actions on the "picas\\_table" DynamoDB table, and attaches the policy to the "picas\\_lambda\\_role" IAM role.\n\nNote that this is just a basic example, and you may need to customize the code depending on your specific requirements. For example, you may want to specify a more fine-grained set of permissions or attach the policy to a different IAM role.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=22, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 2021, 2583, 506, 83927, 1292, 26439, 531,