 73%|███████▎  | 732/1000 [01:46<00:19, 13.56it/s]INFO 09-15 16:13:54 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-15079ebaa52043a197ad9de067ecedf4-0: prompt: '3.1. Machine Learning and Deep Learning in Retrosynthetic Analysis\n\nIn recent years, machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) techniques have emerged as powerful tools to address the limitations of manual retrosynthetic analysis and traditional computational methods. These data-driven approaches can learn complex patterns and relationships from large chemical datasets, enabling more accurate and efficient predictions of retrosynthetic routes (86, 87). This section discusses the application of ML and DL in retrosynthetic analysis, highlighting key advancements and methodologies.\n\n1. Neural network models: Neural networks, particularly deep learning models like convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs), have been applied to predict retrosynthetic routes by learning complex representations of molecular structures and reaction patterns (88, 89). For example, the work by Segler et al. (90) employed a combination of CNNs and RNNs to predict suitable precursors for a given target molecule.\n2. Sequence-to-sequence models: Sequence-to-sequence models, which are typically based on RNNs and long short-term memory (LSTM) networks, have been applied to translate molecular representations of target compounds into precursor molecules, mimicking the process of retrosynthetic disconnection (91, 92). The work by Liu et al. (93) used a sequence-to-sequence model to predict high-level disconnection patterns for a given target molecule.\n3. Graph convolutional networks (GCNs): GCNs have been used to model molecular graphs for predicting retrosynthetic routes, taking advantage of their ability to capture local and global structural features of molecules (94, 95). For instance, Coley et al. (96) developed a graph-convolutional approach to predict retrosynthetic routes by learning a continuous representation of molecular structures and reaction templates.\n4. Reinforcement learning (RL): RL techniques, such as Q-learning and deep Q-networks (DQNs), have been applied to retrosynthetic analysis by modeling the disconnection process as a decision-making problem, where the algorithm iteratively learns the optimal actions to perform at each step (97, 98). The work by Zhou et al. (99) employed a DQN-based approach to navigate the retrosynthetic tree and identify efficient synthetic routes.\n5. Transfer learning: Transfer learning techniques have been used to leverage pre-trained models and knowledge from related tasks to improve the performance of retrosynthetic analysis models, particularly when training data is limited (100, 101). The work by Schwaller et al. (102) demonstrated the effectiveness of transfer learning in predicting reaction templates for retrosynthetic disconnections.\n6. Multi-task learning: Multi-task learning methods, which involve training a single model to perform multiple tasks simultaneously, have been used to improve the accuracy and generalizability of retrosynthetic analysis models by leveraging the shared knowledge between related tasks (103, 104). An example of this approach can be found in the work by Dai et al. (105), where a single model was trained to predict multiple reaction outcomes, such as yield and selectivity, in addition to retrosynthetic routes.\n\nThe application of ML and DL in retrosynthetic analysis has shown significant promise in overcoming the limitations of manual methods and traditional computational approaches, enabling the development of more accurate, efficient, and scalable tools for synthetic route prediction (106, 107).\n\nReferences:\n\n1. Gao, H., Struble, T. J., Coley, C. W., Wang, Y., Green, W. H., & Jensen, K. F. (2020). Using machine learning to predict organic reaction outcomes: Are we there yet? Chemical Science, 11(44), 12119-12130.\n2. Schneider, G. (', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0