 80%|████████  | 802/1000 [01:54<00:28,  6.99it/s]INFO 09-15 16:14:01 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-bf48c7fae8a54f339bc5e922b22b6f14-0: prompt: 'Sure! Using the definition of &#x03B4;j" role="presentation" style="position: relative;">δjδj\\delta\\_j, we can simplify it in terms of the &#x03B4;k" role="presentation" style="position: relative;">δkδk\\delta\\_k terms as:\n\n&#x03B4;j=&#x2211;k=1K&#x03B4;k&#x22C5;wkj&#x22C5;&#x03C3;(zj)&#x22C5;(1&#x2212;&#x03C3;(zj))" role="presentation" style="text-align: center; position: relative;">δj=∑k=1Kδk⋅wkj⋅σ(zj)⋅(1−σ(zj))δj=∑k=1Kδk⋅wkj⋅σ(zj)⋅(1−σ(zj))\\delta\\_j = \\sum\\_{k=1}^K \\delta\\_k \\cdot w\\_{kj} \\cdot \\sigma(z\\_j) \\cdot (1-\\sigma(z\\_j))\n\nThis expression tells us that the error term for a neuron in the j-layer can be expressed as a weighted sum of the error terms for the neurons in the k-layer that receive input from the j-layer, where the weights are given by the corresponding weights of the connections between the j-layer neuron and the k-layer neuron, and scaled by the slope of the sigmoid function at the weighted sum of the j-layer neuron.\n\nIn other words, the error in the j-th neuron is a function of the errors in the k-layer neurons that it connects to. This makes sense, since the activation of the j-th neuron depends on the activations of the neurons in the i-layer that connect to it, and the errors in the k-layer neurons that connect to it.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=31, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2, 49190, 236888, 10352, 506, 8514, 529, 62307, 236781, 236771, 236800, 236799, 236812, 23679