 84%|████████▍ | 839/1000 [01:58<00:15, 10.37it/s]INFO 09-15 16:14:06 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-517776220152476c92bddec48875d1ed-0: prompt: "You can use the `rolling.apply()` method to apply any custom function to the rolling window of the data. To calculate the root mean squared of the window, you can use the `np.sqrt(np.mean(np.square(window)))` function.\n```\nsmoothed_emg = pd.Series(filtered_emg).rolling(window=window, center=False, min_periods=1).apply(lambda window: np.sqrt(np.mean(np.square(window))))\n```\nThis will calculate the root mean squared of the window, for each window.\n\nAlso, you can check if the window value is larger than the filtered\\_emg data, then you can set the window value to the length of the filtered\\_emg data.\n```\nif window > len(filtered_emg):\n    window = len(filtered_emg)\n    smoothed_emg = pd.Series(filtered_emg).rolling(window=window, center=False, min_periods=1).apply(lambda window: np.sqrt(np.mean(np.square(window))))\n```\nHere is the full updated code:\n```\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy import signal\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndef analyze_emg_data(file_name, sample_rate=2000, window=0.5):\n    # Import data from excel sheet\n    data = pd.read_excel(file_name)\n    time = data['Time']\n    emg = data['EMG']\n\n    # Apply 2nd order bandpass Butterworth filter\n    nyquist = 0.5 * sample_rate\n    high = 20 / nyquist\n    low = 500 / nyquist\n    b, a = signal.butter(2, [high, low], btype='band')\n    filtered_emg = signal.filtfilt(b, a, emg)\n\n    # Apply 2nd order notch Butterworth filter\n    notch\n```", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=14, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True